{'Name': 'future_window_distribution', 'Title': 'Future-Window Distribution Prediction for Autoregressive Representation Learning', 'Experiment': 'Add an optional auxiliary future-window distribution objective to GPT. In GPT.__init__, create a lightweight Linear head from n_embd to vocab_size, separate from the tied next-token lm_head. Modify GPT.forward to optionally return hidden states or compute an auxiliary loss when targets are provided. For each sequence position during training, build a soft target distribution from the next W characters, for example W in {8, 16, 32}, by counting target token IDs in that future window and normalizing; invalid tail positions are masked out. Compute auxiliary loss as soft cross-entropy or KL divergence between log_softmax(aux_logits) and the future-window distribution. Train with total_loss = next_token_loss + alpha * future_window_loss, using alpha values such as 0.05 and 0.1. Keep estimate_loss, validation, final reporting, and generation based on the standard next-character lm_head for comparability. Compare against the baseline across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8 using best validation loss, convergence speed, training-time overhead, inference tokens per second, generated samples, and an ablation over window size. The hypothesis is that predicting coarse future token statistics provides a stable medium-range learning signal that improves sample efficiency and representation quality without requiring exact multi-step prediction.', 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 8, 'Novelty': 6}
