{'Name': 'future_window_distribution', 'Title': 'Future-Window Distribution Prediction for Autoregressive Representation Learning', 'Experiment': 'Add a simple auxiliary objective that asks each hidden state to predict the distribution of characters appearing in its next local future window. In GPT.__init__, add an optional future_window_head = nn.Linear(n_embd, vocab_size, bias=False). In GPT.forward, keep the standard tied lm_head and next-character cross-entropy unchanged, but when targets are provided and the auxiliary option is enabled, also compute aux_logits = future_window_head(x). Build a soft target distribution for each position by counting token IDs in targets[:, t:t+W], where W is the future window size, normalizing by the number of valid positions. Compute the auxiliary loss as soft cross-entropy between log_softmax(aux_logits) and this future-window distribution, averaged over batch and sequence positions. Train with total_loss = next_loss + alpha * future_window_loss, using W=16 and alpha=0.1 as the main setting, with small ablations W in {8, 32} and alpha in {0.05, 0.1}. Keep estimate_loss, validation, final reporting, and generation based on the ordinary next-character loss and standard lm_head for comparability. Compare against the baseline across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8 using best validation loss, convergence speed, total training time, inference tokens per second, generated samples, and auxiliary-head parameter overhead.', 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 9, 'Novelty': 6}
