{'Name': 'next_span_contrastive', 'Title': 'Next-Span Contrastive Prediction for Chunk-Aware Autoregressive Modeling', 'Experiment': 'Add a lightweight contrastive auxiliary objective that trains hidden states to identify their true upcoming character span among in-batch negatives. Extend GPTConfig with use_span_contrastive, span_len, num_span_anchors, span_loss_weight, span_temperature, and span_warmup. In GPT.__init__, add an anchor projection, a span projection, and learned relative span position embeddings. In GPT.forward, keep the standard next-character lm_head and cross-entropy unchanged. When targets are provided and the model is in training mode, randomly sample num_span_anchors valid anchor positions where a future span of length span_len is available. For each anchor, encode the true future span targets[:, anchor:anchor+span_len] using the tied token embedding table plus relative span embeddings, mean-pool the span representation, and project it. Project the hidden state x[:, anchor, :], L2-normalize both projections, compute batch-by-batch similarities divided by span_temperature, and apply InfoNCE cross-entropy with diagonal positives. Train with total_loss = next_token_loss + alpha_t * mean(span_losses), where alpha_t ramps from 0 to span_loss_weight after warmup. Because the auxiliary loss is only active in training mode, estimate_loss, validation, final reporting, and generation remain based on ordinary next-character loss. Add an optional small diagnostic that reports next-span retrieval accuracy on a few validation batches. Compare the baseline to span contrastive training with span_len=16, num_span_anchors=2, span_loss_weight=0.05, and a small ablation at span_loss_weight=0.1 across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Report best validation loss, convergence speed, total training time, inference tokens per second, generated samples, retrieval accuracy, and auxiliary parameter overhead.', 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 8, 'Novelty': 6}
