{'Name': 'sparse_ngram_distillation', 'Title': 'Sparse N-Gram Teacher Distillation for Sample-Efficient Character Transformers', 'Experiment': "Build a fixed smoothed backoff n-gram teacher from each dataset's train.bin before training, using orders up to 5 and storing count dictionaries keyed by compact integer contexts. During training, keep the standard next-character cross-entropy loss unchanged. After warmup_iters, sample a small subset of token positions from each batch, query the highest-confidence available n-gram distribution for those contexts, keep only its top-k next-character probabilities, and add a sparse KL distillation term between the student's logits at those positions and the teacher's top-k distribution. Gate the auxiliary loss off when the teacher context count is below a minimum threshold, and ramp the loss weight alpha_t from 0 to alpha over 1000 iterations. Validation, generation, and final reporting remain based on ordinary next-character cross-entropy with no n-gram teacher at inference. Compare baseline training against max_order=3 and max_order=5 teachers, top_k values 8 and 16, and alpha values 0.05 and 0.1 across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Report best validation loss, early convergence speed, train-time overhead, inference tokens per second, generated samples, and diagnostics for teacher coverage and student-teacher agreement.", 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 9, 'Novelty': 6}
