{'Name': 'learned_local_pointer_cache', 'Title': 'Learned Local Pointer Caches for Copy-Aware Character Language Modeling', 'Experiment': "Add an optional learned local pointer-cache output path to GPT. Extend GPTConfig with use_pointer_cache, pointer_window, pointer_temperature, and pointer_gate_bias. In GPT.__init__, add small linear projections for pointer queries and keys plus a scalar or per-token gate head. In GPT.forward, after the final hidden states x are computed, keep the standard lm_head logits and next-character cross-entropy objective. When pointer cache is enabled, compute causal similarities between each position's query and previous key states within pointer_window, mask future positions and optionally positions outside the window, apply softmax over valid previous positions, and scatter-sum that attention mass into a vocabulary-sized cache probability distribution according to the observed input tokens at those previous positions. Convert lm_head logits to probabilities, blend them with the cache distribution using sigmoid(gate_head(x)), and compute negative log likelihood of targets from the blended distribution with ignore_index support. For numerical stability, clamp probabilities before log. Generation uses the same blended distribution at the final position, so no separate sampling code path is needed beyond allowing GPT.generate to consume probabilities or blended logits. Compare the baseline against pointer_window values 32, 64, and 128, and report best validation loss, convergence speed, total train time, inference tokens per second, generated samples, average gate value, copy-hit rate, and parameter overhead across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Include an ablation with a fixed non-learned gate to separate the value of the cache distribution from the learned routing mechanism.", 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 8, 'Novelty': 6}
