{'Name': 'residual_update_budgeting', 'Title': 'Residual Update Budgeting for Selective Information Flow in Autoregressive Transformers', 'Experiment': 'Add an optional learned residual-update budget to Transformer blocks. Extend GPTConfig with use_update_budgeting, update_budget_weight, target_update_rate, update_gate_temperature, and update_budget_ramp_iters. In Block.__init__, add lightweight scalar gate heads for the attention and MLP residual branches, for example nn.Linear(n_embd, 1), initialized with a positive bias so training starts close to the baseline. In Block.forward, compute gate_attn = sigmoid(gate_head_attn(ln_1(x)) / temperature) and gate_mlp = sigmoid(gate_head_mlp(ln_2(x)) / temperature), then apply x = x + gate_attn * attn(ln_1(x)) and x = x + gate_mlp * mlp(ln_2(x)) when enabled. Collect mean gate values across layers and positions. In the training loop, keep ordinary next-character cross-entropy as the main loss and add a ramped budget penalty after warmup_iters: budget_loss = max(0, mean_gate - target_update_rate)^2, using total_loss = ce_loss + alpha_t * budget_loss. Keep validation, generation, and final reporting based on standard next-character loss with the learned gates active. Compare the baseline against target_update_rate values such as 0.5, 0.7, and 0.9 with update_budget_weight values 0.01 and 0.05 across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Report best validation loss, convergence speed, total train time, inference tokens per second, generated samples, average gate usage by layer, gate usage by token loss quantile, and whether gates concentrate on high-uncertainty or boundary-like character positions.', 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 9, 'Novelty': 6}
