{'Name': 'context_support_tempering', 'Title': 'Context Support-Aware Target Tempering for Memorization-Resistant Character Language Models', 'Experiment': "Before training each dataset, build train-only fixed-size count-min or rolling-hash sketches for long causal context suffixes such as lengths 8, 16, and 32, plus a simple unigram or short-context continuation prior. Modify GPT.forward or the training loop to compute per-token cross-entropy with reduction='none'. After warmup_iters, query the approximate support count of each token position's visible context from X, convert low support counts into a per-position smoothing value epsilon, and train with a tempered objective: (1 - epsilon) * one_hot_nll + epsilon * short_prior_cross_entropy, normalizing over batch-time positions. High-support contexts receive epsilon near zero, so common predictable patterns are trained exactly as in the baseline. Keep estimate_loss, final reporting, and generation based on ordinary one-hot next-character loss for comparability. Add diagnostics for train-validation loss gap, validation calibration by confidence bucket, generated n-gram copy rate against train.bin, loss stratified by context support, and sample diversity. Compare the baseline, uniform label smoothing, and support-aware tempering across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8 with epsilon_max values such as 0.05, 0.1, and 0.2.", 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 8, 'Novelty': 7}
