{'Name': 'rare_context_tempering', 'Title': 'Rare-Context Target Tempering for Memorization-Resistant Character Language Models', 'Experiment': "Before training each dataset, build a train-only fixed-size hash table of clipped support counts for causal context suffixes of length L, using rolling hashes over train.bin and no validation data. In get_batch or the training loop, estimate the support count for each batch-time position from the visible context suffix ending at the current input token, ignoring positions with fewer than L visible tokens. Modify GPT.forward or the training loop to compute per-token cross-entropy with reduction='none' and log probabilities. After warmup_iters, convert support counts into a per-position smoothing value epsilon_t = epsilon_max * max(0, support_threshold - count) / support_threshold, so rare contexts receive stronger smoothing and frequent contexts remain nearly baseline. Train with loss = mean((1 - epsilon_t) * one_hot_nll + epsilon_t * uniform_nll), where uniform_nll is the negative mean log probability over the vocabulary. Keep estimate_loss, best validation loss, final reporting, inference speed, and generation based on ordinary one-hot next-character loss for comparability. Compare the baseline, fixed uniform label smoothing, and rare-context tempering with L in {8, 16}, support_threshold values such as 2 and 4, and epsilon_max in {0.05, 0.1, 0.2} across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Report best validation loss, train-validation gap, calibration by confidence bucket, loss stratified by context support, generated repeated n-gram rate, and approximate train-substring copy rate.", 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 9, 'Novelty': 7}

