{'Name': 'causal_transition_cache', 'Title': 'Causal Transition Caches for In-Context Analogical Language Modeling', 'Experiment': 'Add an optional strictly causal neural transition-cache output path to GPT. Extend GPTConfig with use_transition_cache, transition_cache_window, transition_cache_temperature, transition_cache_gate_bias, and transition_cache_eval_mode. In GPT.__init__, add lightweight query and key projections from final hidden states plus a scalar gate head initialized with a negative bias so training starts close to the baseline. In GPT.forward, compute ordinary lm_head logits and hidden states as usual. When the transition cache is enabled, compare each query hidden state h_t only to earlier key states h_s with s < t and t - s <= transition_cache_window. The value associated with key position s is the observed next input token idx[:, s + 1], which is visible at query position t when s < t, so there is no target leakage. Softmax the masked similarities, scatter-add the resulting mass into a vocabulary-sized cache distribution over these historical continuation tokens, and blend it with the neural softmax distribution using sigmoid(gate_head(h_t)). For positions with no valid earlier transition, force the gate to zero. Compute next-character negative log likelihood from the blended probabilities while also logging base-only loss, cache-only loss where valid, average gate value, target-in-cache rate, and gate value versus cache confidence. For targets=None generation, compute the same cache from the generated prefix and return log blended probabilities for compatibility with the existing temperature, top_k, and sampling code. Compare the baseline against transition_cache_window values 32, 64, and 128, plus ablations with a fixed scalar gate and with detached cache similarities, across shakespeare_char, enwik8, and text8. Report best blended validation loss, base-only validation loss, convergence speed, total train time, inference tokens per second with cache active, generated samples, average gate usage, target-in-cache rate, and whether gains concentrate on repeated or structurally recurring contexts.', 'Interestingness': 8, 'Feasibility': 8, 'Novelty': 7}
